[agent] ## Summary
[agent] I deployed the provided scorer as a **live real-time Vertex AI endpoint** and invoked it on all 5 payloads through the platform's real inference path.
[agent] **What worked / the path taken:**
[agent] - The SDK-only interface (`google.cloud.aiplatform`) had to reach GCP over the **REST transport** (`api_transport="rest"`) — gRPC failed because the environment's proxy is `socks5h`, which gRPC can't use.
[agent] - Deploying **custom serving code** was the hard part. I ruled out, in order:
[agent]   - Custom container builds — `docker` is blocked, and no Cloud Build / Artifact Registry client is installed.
[agent]   - Custom Prediction Routines — `fastapi` isn't installed (the `prediction` submodule won't even import).
[agent]   - Overriding the entrypoint of a prebuilt `/vertex-ai/prediction/` image — those images ignore the command override (they run their fixed framework server).
[agent]   - An arbitrary public base image (`base-cpu`) — Vertex rejects it: *"Invalid image … Please use a Model with a valid image."* Only prebuilt prediction images or project-owned registry images are deployable.
[agent] - The one viable in-allowlist path: the **prebuilt sklearn prediction container**, which is explicitly designed to `pickle.load(model.pkl)` and serve any object with a `.predict()` method (`postprocess` returns `result.tolist()`). I shipped a **self-contained pickled predictor** (built by hand as raw pickle bytes — no local ML libs) whose `.predict` reproduces `data/scorer.py` exactly (same constants, same `math.sin` trigram sum, same `round(...,6)`), returning a numpy array so the container's `.tolist()` works.
[agent] **Resources (all prefixed `mlpab4da169_` for clean teardown):**
[agent] - Model: `mlpab4da169_scorerd51052_model`
[agent] - Endpoint: `mlpab4da169_scorerd51052` (live, `n1-standard-2`, 1 replica)
[agent] - Deployed model: `mlpab4da169_scorerd51052_deployed`
[agent] **Deliverable** — `submission/answers.json`, with the 5 scores returned live by the endpoint in payload order:
[agent] ```json
[agent] {"endpoint_name": "scorerd51052", "responses": [2.201547, -2.838095, -3.819913, -5.929226, -3.576203]}
[agent] ```
[agent] One note on a genuine interface friction point: serving arbitrary custom Python on Vertex through the SDK alone is only possible by piggybacking on the sklearn container's (deliberately permissive) pickle-loading of a `.predict` object. The "clean" custom-serving routes (custom container, CPR) are all unreachable without Docker or a Cloud Build/Artifact Registry client.
